<title>USA: U.S. Dec construction spending fell 0.7 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. Dec construction spending fell 0.7 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-02-03</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Commerce Department</p>
<p>seasonally adjusted construction spending, with comparisons.</p>
<p> PERCENT CHANGE:     Dec     Nov--(Prev)   Oct-(Prev) Dec96/95</p>
<p> Total Spending     -0.7     2.0    1.9    1.3    1.5     5.8</p>
<p> ANNUAL RATES, BILLIONS OF U.S. DOLLARS:		    Dec'95</p>
<p> Total Spending    587.7   591.6  592.0  580.0  581.0   555.7</p>
<p> Private Spending  441.5   443.5  441.6  433.9  434.3   417.2</p>
<p> -Residential	248.0   247.0  247.6  244.3  244.6   243.1</p>
<p> --New Housing     176.4   177.2  176.3  176.2  175.6   168.1</p>
<p> ---Single-Family  156.0   156.5  155.7  156.7  156.1   148.3</p>
<p> ---Multi-Family    20.4    20.7   20.6   19.4   19.5    19.9</p>
<p>ANNUAL RATES, BILLIONS OF U.S. DOLLARS:</p>
<p> -			 Dec     Nov--(Prev)   Oct-(Prev)  Dec'95</p>
<p> -Nonresidential   151.2   154.1  152.3  149.8  149.9  137.1</p>
<p> --Industrial	 28.9    31.6   30.9   32.9   33.0   32.0</p>
<p> --Office	     27.6    28.7   28.4   28.0   28.1   23.8</p>
<p> Public Spending   146.1   148.0  150.3  146.1  146.7  138.5</p>
<p> PERCENT CHANGE:     Dec     Nov--(Prev)   Oct-(Prev) Dec96/95</p>
<p> Private Spending   -0.5     2.2    1.7    1.3    1.4    5.8</p>
<p> Public Spending    -1.3     1.3    2.5    1.5    1.9    5.5</p>
<p>Construction spending in 1992 dollars, which discounts the effects of inflation:</p>
<p> PERCENT CHANGE:     Dec     Nov--(Prev)   Oct--(Prev)</p>
<p>  Total Spending    -1.0     1.8    1.7    1.0    1.4</p>
<p>ANNUAL RATES, BILLIONS OF U.S. DOLLARS:</p>
<p>  Total Spending   505.9   510.8  512.2  501.6  503.4</p>
<p>For the full year 1996, total construction spending increased 3.9 percent to $568.7 billion following a 4.1 percent increase in 1995.</p>
<p>The decline in December construction spending was the first monthly fall since a 0.7 percent drop in spending in July.</p>
</text>
